INT. LIBRARY OF THE OLD MILL HOUSE - EVENING

A long, low room under a timbered ceiling. Shelves of leather books, a fire crackling in a stone hearth, a rain-streaked window overlooking the mill race. A grandfather clock stands against the wall, stopped at ten past four.

INSPECTOR CORNELIA VANDERHOEK-ASANTE (50s, gray tweed, a pipe she never lights) paces slowly, studying the shelves. Two suspects sit in leather chairs by the fire, each with a glass of sherry untouched at their elbow.

LADY OTTILIE FAIRBROTHER-ZULU (70s, silver rings on every finger) sits bolt upright, spine like a ruler. Beside her, the housekeeper, MRS. AGATHA PENHALIGON-DUBOIS (60s, apron still tied, hands folded in her lap) stares at the floor.

CORNELIA
Thank you all for waiting.

OTTILIE
We had little choice, Inspector.

CORNELIA
No. You didn't.

She stops at a shelf. Runs a finger along the spines. Pulls one out, holds it up, replaces it.

OTTILIE
This is absurd. My brother Reginald fell down the stairs.

CORNELIA
He did. Eventually.

MRS. PENHALIGON
(softly)
The poor man.

CORNELIA
Mrs. Penhaligon, you found the body at six?

MRS. PENHALIGON
Quarter to six, ma'am. The clock was already stopped.

CORNELIA
At ten past four.

OTTILIE
It's been stopped for years.

CORNELIA
No, Lady Fairbrother. It was stopped this morning. The pendulum is still warm.

Silence. The fire pops. Ottilie's hand tightens on the arm of her chair.

CORNELIA (CONT'D)
Someone stopped it at ten past four to give themselves an alibi.

OTTILIE
Who would do that?

CORNELIA
Someone who forgot the clock has a second winding key.

The door opens, and a young constable, DENNIS HOLLOWAY-PARK (20s, rain-slick cape), comes in holding a small brass key in a handkerchief.

DENNIS
Found it, Inspector. In the pocket of the dead man's dressing gown.

CORNELIA
Thank you, Constable.

She takes the key, turns it over, and looks at each of them in turn. Ottilie meets her gaze. And Mrs. Penhaligon, very slowly, lifts her eyes.

MRS. PENHALIGON
It's mine, ma'am.

OTTILIE
Agatha --

MRS. PENHALIGON
It was always going to come to this, my lady.

CORNELIA
(gently)
Mrs. Penhaligon, would you come with me?

She nods, rises, smooths her apron, and walks to the door without looking back.
